o indexes exist beyond the primary keys.\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email) = '...'` wraps the column in a function. A plain index on `email` can't be used, so the database runs `lower()` on every row.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, unless `orders.user_id` is indexed.** The join on `o.user_id = u.id` has nothing to seek on. The planner either scans `orders` for the matching user or hashes the whole table.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, so no covering index can serve the query. It also returns duplicate column names such as `id` and `created_at`, which breaks or confuses some clients.\n\n**Fixes**\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Expression index, so the lower(email) predicate becomes an index seek.\n--    Make it UNIQUE if emails are unique case-insensitively.\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Index the join key so orders are found by seek.\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nRewritten query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total,   -- list only the columns you need\n       u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower('a@example.org');\n```\n\nThe `lower()` call must match the index expression exactly. Lowercasing the parameter as well means the caller doesn't have to.\n\n**Better long-term option:** make email case-insensitive at the column level, so queries can use a plain `WHERE email = ...`.\n- In Postgres, use the `citext` type, or store emails lowercased on write with a check constraint.\n- Then a plain unique index on `email` is enough, and there's no function to match.\n\n**Other notes**\n- If the real query needs only a few order columns, `INCLUDE` them in the `orders` index (`(user_id) INCLUDE (...)`) to allow index-only scans.\n- On MySQL 8, the equivalent is a functional index: `((lower(email)))`. On MySQL with a case-insensitive collation, `lower()` isn't needed at all.\n- Confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. You want an index scan on `users` and an index or bitmap scan on `orders`, not a `Seq Scan`.\n\nIf you tell me the engine and share the schema, I can tighten the column list and the index definitions.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}